1
00:00:00,631 --> 00:00:06,776
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the audio on some notes on the practice of mindfulness of the body.

2
00:00:08,297 --> 00:00:21,747
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll read a paragraph also, that is relevant, based on Venerable Aryavamsa's translations, that... the Kāyā-gatā-sati.

3
00:00:22,968 --> 00:00:23,728
[SPEAKER_00]: So, dig in in Kāyā-34.

4
00:00:27,131 --> 00:00:33,254
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a bit that says, which is the one thing that should be developed?

5
00:00:33,314 --> 00:00:38,176
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the Buddha answers, mindfulness of the body, endowed with pleasure.

6
00:00:39,497 --> 00:00:41,858
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the one thing that should be developed.

7
00:00:44,179 --> 00:00:53,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, further in Majjhima Nikaya 119, the Buddha goes on talking about the mindfulness of the body in relation to the

8
00:00:56,811 --> 00:00:57,952
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness of breathing.

9
00:01:00,413 --> 00:01:01,834
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it goes like this.

10
00:01:02,374 --> 00:01:10,658
[SPEAKER_00]: "...and bhikkhus, developed in what way, made much of in what way, is mindfulness of the body of much fruit, of much benefit.

11
00:01:12,579 --> 00:01:25,005
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu gone to the forest, or gone to the root of a tree, or gone to an empty hut, sits down, having crossed his legs, with the intention of a straight back, having set up mindfulness in front.

12
00:01:26,657 --> 00:01:27,618
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the important bit.

13
00:01:27,959 --> 00:01:29,020
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness in front.

14
00:01:30,401 --> 00:01:31,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite mindfully, he breathes in.

15
00:01:32,344 --> 00:01:33,966
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite mindfully, he breathes out.

16
00:01:34,947 --> 00:01:37,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Breathing in long, he understands I'm breathing in long.

17
00:01:38,051 --> 00:01:40,734
[SPEAKER_00]: Breathing out long, he understands I'm breathing out long.

18
00:01:41,475 --> 00:01:43,717
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is the bit that I wanted to mention a few points.

19
00:01:43,998 --> 00:01:45,820
[SPEAKER_00]: It's, eh... he understands.

20
00:01:48,540 --> 00:02:08,930
[SPEAKER_00]: Based on the other talks that we gave on peripheral awareness or awareness, done properly, pretty much, I wanted to point out here that when it says here, breathing in long, he understands that he's breathing in long.

21
00:02:09,990 --> 00:02:12,572
[SPEAKER_00]: That is not something that he attends.

22
00:02:13,532 --> 00:02:15,233
[SPEAKER_00]: That is something that he knows.

23
00:02:16,472 --> 00:02:19,174
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what he attends, it's what's in front.

24
00:02:19,575 --> 00:02:24,279
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what I said, why this is important beforehand, when I said mindfulness.

25
00:02:24,299 --> 00:02:26,440
[SPEAKER_00]: He sets up his mindfulness in front.

26
00:02:28,262 --> 00:02:32,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Means, he surmounts his experience as a whole, puts it in front, so to speak.

27
00:02:32,345 --> 00:02:33,466
[SPEAKER_00]: He attends to that.

28
00:02:34,988 --> 00:02:40,833
[SPEAKER_00]: And while that thing is enduring, and in this case, that thing is breathing, he knows

29
00:02:41,692 --> 00:02:44,554
[SPEAKER_00]: What is enduring without having to attend it?

30
00:02:45,594 --> 00:02:59,942
[SPEAKER_00]: As it was mentioned in the essay that I wrote, also called Peripheral Awareness, people can find it on our website, the knowledge is always peripheral.

31
00:03:00,162 --> 00:03:00,942
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how it works.

32
00:03:01,823 --> 00:03:06,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you attend to it, you attend to the particular exemplification of that.

33
00:03:07,721 --> 00:03:16,187
[SPEAKER_00]: But arriving in the knowledge, arriving in the insight, arriving in the understanding of nature of things, that's done at the background of whatever you're attending.

34
00:03:16,207 --> 00:03:23,472
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you cannot, through your pure attention, and pure observation, and pure perception, arrive at knowledge.

35
00:03:26,354 --> 00:03:32,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowledge is developed when you understand the nature of what you're perceiving, attending, and so on.

36
00:03:34,265 --> 00:03:36,366
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why... that's how mindfulness is done.

37
00:03:36,386 --> 00:03:47,850
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why all... as again, as we've done in other talks, explained peripheral awareness based on the practice of mindfulness of the body, the referencing, anchoring, no matter how you translate it.

38
00:03:48,550 --> 00:03:53,032
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is, you establish your mind on the peripheral image while you're attending something else.

39
00:03:53,792 --> 00:04:01,775
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you learn how to not try to attend that which you're supposed to understand, but instead, discern it.

40
00:04:02,591 --> 00:04:08,713
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to whatever you set up in front of you as your practice.

41
00:04:09,333 --> 00:04:15,395
[SPEAKER_00]: And in this case, and also it's important to know that in this case, it's... a person needs to be physically secluded.

42
00:04:15,435 --> 00:04:21,157
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't do this practice surrounded by the people, no matter how quiet and silent they are.

43
00:04:21,237 --> 00:04:26,779
[SPEAKER_00]: You need actual physical seclusion, physically removed from the presence of another.

44
00:04:27,620 --> 00:04:30,741
[SPEAKER_00]: A phenomenon of another needs to be absent in your experience.

45
00:04:30,781 --> 00:04:31,721
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to be alone.

46
00:04:32,920 --> 00:04:36,289
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like going to the root of a tree, a forest, an empty hut.

47
00:04:37,412 --> 00:04:39,217
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not just for the romantic value.

48
00:04:43,073 --> 00:04:48,417
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you're in such a seclusion, you sit down, you set up mindfulness to the fore.

49
00:04:48,437 --> 00:04:50,519
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no more specific instruction.

50
00:04:50,639 --> 00:04:53,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, you watch the tip of your nostril and... No.

51
00:04:53,541 --> 00:04:55,382
[SPEAKER_00]: You bring the mindfulness to forward.

52
00:04:55,983 --> 00:04:58,024
[SPEAKER_00]: And what you bring mindfulness...

53
00:04:58,825 --> 00:05:03,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry, of what mindfulness forward is brought, it's basically of your experience as a whole.

54
00:05:04,789 --> 00:05:05,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Experience of breathing.

55
00:05:06,030 --> 00:05:06,711
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you're doing.

56
00:05:06,731 --> 00:05:08,132
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm sitting down and I'm breathing.

57
00:05:08,991 --> 00:05:13,354
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can say you attend to that, just in a general manner.

58
00:05:14,715 --> 00:05:22,220
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, while you're doing it, you keep a corner of your eye, so to speak, to the knowledge of what you are doing.

59
00:05:23,101 --> 00:05:26,103
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, it's quite pointed and stressed out here.

60
00:05:26,863 --> 00:05:30,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Breathing in long, he understands, I'm breathing in long.

61
00:05:31,426 --> 00:05:36,870
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't have to stop breathing or stop attending mindfulness to the four that he has brought, the attention to the four.

62
00:05:38,182 --> 00:05:44,884
[SPEAKER_00]: In front of him, in order to understand that he's breathing, he simply understands that which he's doing.

63
00:05:46,144 --> 00:05:51,845
[SPEAKER_00]: And he understands it by not trying to attend the nature of what he's doing, but by discerning it.

64
00:05:52,765 --> 00:05:55,246
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that's the entire point I'm making here.

65
00:05:57,427 --> 00:05:59,587
[SPEAKER_00]: So, breathing in long, he understands he's breathing in long.

66
00:05:59,987 --> 00:06:02,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Breathing out long, he understands he's breathing out long.

67
00:06:03,370 --> 00:06:05,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Breathing in short, he understands.

68
00:06:05,692 --> 00:06:06,733
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm breathing in short.

69
00:06:07,413 --> 00:06:08,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Breathing out short, he understands.

70
00:06:09,195 --> 00:06:13,719
[SPEAKER_00]: It means, for any aspect, he doesn't mean he has to catch, oh, I'm in, out, in, out.

71
00:06:14,419 --> 00:06:21,766
[SPEAKER_00]: It means that, eh... at any point of the duration of in and out breath, he understands the nature of it.

72
00:06:23,387 --> 00:06:29,092
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is the way to describe it, that he's fully aware, without attending it directly.

73
00:06:29,847 --> 00:06:36,587
[SPEAKER_00]: Because what he attends directly is that which he has put in front, which is not the understanding of what he is doing in front.

74
00:06:38,864 --> 00:06:46,708
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, that's how he then starts on that peripheral level, including the phenomenon of this whole body that he did put in the front initially.

75
00:06:47,348 --> 00:06:48,389
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he trains thus.

76
00:06:49,069 --> 00:06:51,190
[SPEAKER_00]: I will breed out experiencing the whole body.

77
00:06:51,750 --> 00:06:52,390
[SPEAKER_00]: He trains thus.

78
00:06:52,570 --> 00:06:57,233
[SPEAKER_00]: I will breed out calming the bodily determinations, the bodily saṅkhara.

79
00:06:57,333 --> 00:07:03,896
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, nature of saṅkhara, it's always that upon which something else depends on.

80
00:07:04,736 --> 00:07:08,398
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, nature of saṅkhara is always to be in the background.

81
00:07:11,545 --> 00:07:17,847
[SPEAKER_00]: The best example of this is the dependence of nāmarūpa and viññāṇa, viññāṇa and nāmarūpa, as the Buddha described it.

82
00:07:17,947 --> 00:07:22,089
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you look at nāmarūpa, it's held by... it's determined by viññāṇa.

83
00:07:22,109 --> 00:07:29,972
[SPEAKER_00]: If you look at viññāṇa, it's determined by nāmarūpa, which means determination, saṅkhāra, is always opposite of where you look.

84
00:07:30,412 --> 00:07:32,153
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you cannot see it directly.

85
00:07:32,313 --> 00:07:37,315
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot see it with with your attention, with your intention, because they're already determined.

86
00:07:43,236 --> 00:07:52,144
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point is to recognize, to start developing this indirect vision of peripheral or indirect vision of proper mindfulness.

87
00:07:53,386 --> 00:07:54,767
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason I'm insisting on this

88
00:07:55,895 --> 00:08:00,557
[SPEAKER_00]: description of peripheral is because... well, because of the many misconceptions today.

89
00:08:00,677 --> 00:08:04,358
[SPEAKER_00]: People... people do their mindfulness by attending moment to moment to moment to moment.

90
00:08:04,378 --> 00:08:10,680
[SPEAKER_00]: There's absolutely no understanding, no awareness of intentionality, eh... developed behind.

91
00:08:11,060 --> 00:08:14,061
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, it's actually implicit, even in those misguided acts.

92
00:08:16,222 --> 00:08:25,265
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he... he trains in a way that he will, while he's breathing in, he will be studying these

93
00:08:26,658 --> 00:08:28,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Peripheral body as a determination.

94
00:08:29,399 --> 00:08:30,459
[SPEAKER_00]: And that means exactly that.

95
00:08:30,879 --> 00:08:48,623
[SPEAKER_00]: The body that you, well, eventually learn to discern on the peripheral level, without needing to directly look at it and start feeling and touching and getting your sensations going and so on, which is usually how people practice meditation, observing sensation in this body right in front of me.

96
00:08:48,683 --> 00:08:48,843
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

97
00:08:49,403 --> 00:08:51,984
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to discern the body on the peripheral level.

98
00:08:53,487 --> 00:09:01,463
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's that body because of which you are able to observe all these sensations and whatnot right in front of you.

99
00:09:02,430 --> 00:09:11,855
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it's said in that Ānāpānasati Sutta, that many translators and scholars get confused over how to translate it, when it says, that is body among bodies.

100
00:09:12,295 --> 00:09:16,697
[SPEAKER_00]: That phenomena stands basically for what your body is at that present time.

101
00:09:17,117 --> 00:09:18,298
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no other body.

102
00:09:18,738 --> 00:09:24,081
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that body of breath they're trying to translate, or maybe means this, maybe means body, means exactly that.

103
00:09:24,877 --> 00:09:28,759
[SPEAKER_00]: means body discerned peripherally, not body attended directly.

104
00:09:29,359 --> 00:09:30,900
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's a fundamental difference.

105
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:32,400
[SPEAKER_00]: One of them is object of your attention.

106
00:09:32,460 --> 00:09:35,022
[SPEAKER_00]: The other one is saṅkhara of your experience as a whole.

107
00:09:35,582 --> 00:09:49,908
[SPEAKER_00]: And it is that saṅkhara of the experience as a whole that the Buddha talks about as the value of single mindfulness that you need to develop that will result in basically full liberation and supernatural powers or whatever else, if there is an opening for that, of course.

108
00:09:51,209 --> 00:09:52,810
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's exactly what this points out.

109
00:09:53,568 --> 00:09:59,271
[SPEAKER_00]: He trains in a way that he will breathe in, discerning these bodily determinations.

110
00:10:00,091 --> 00:10:08,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Not looking at the body, not chasing sensations, but discerning that peripheral, remote, ambiguous image of the body there.

111
00:10:09,667 --> 00:10:11,788
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's also what it says in Satipaṭṭhāna Sutta.

112
00:10:11,828 --> 00:10:13,429
[SPEAKER_00]: I mentioned that in other talks.

113
00:10:14,510 --> 00:10:22,755
[SPEAKER_00]: That... where he... where the Buddha says he practices mindfulness of the body internally, externally and so on.

114
00:10:23,216 --> 00:10:32,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Or he discerns... or he practices mindfulness to the extent necessary for the final knowledge in relation to he knows the body is there.

115
00:10:34,095 --> 00:10:40,524
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he develops mindfulness of the knowledge, the body is there, to the extent necessary for final knowledge.

116
00:10:40,585 --> 00:10:41,706
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's it.

117
00:10:41,746 --> 00:10:45,352
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's that body there, is that peripheral image of the body.

118
00:10:46,393 --> 00:10:49,117
[SPEAKER_00]: Which, if you attend directly, it just becomes an abstract thought.

119
00:10:50,028 --> 00:11:04,525
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you see it with proper mindfulness, with a cor... with... indirectly, without needing to attend it, then, actually, it stands for a phenomenon which is a determination of your body that you can know right here in front of you, that you can see, touch, perceive, and so on.

120
00:11:10,906 --> 00:11:21,749
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, this passage goes on in saying, for one dwelling in this way, vigilant, ardent, resolute, any memories and thoughts connected with home are abandoned.

121
00:11:22,169 --> 00:11:39,835
[SPEAKER_00]: With abandoning of these internally, and by the way, internally, is also something that can almost always, there are some exceptions though, but there will be a digression here, but internally can almost always be used

122
00:11:41,851 --> 00:11:48,878
[SPEAKER_00]: To describe that peripheral, that which you cannot directly attend, which you attend becomes external, pretty much, even if it's internal things.

123
00:11:49,318 --> 00:11:50,820
[SPEAKER_00]: But anyway, let's not get into that here.

124
00:11:51,500 --> 00:11:55,024
[SPEAKER_00]: So, with the abandoning of these internally, the mind stands still.

125
00:11:56,505 --> 00:12:00,789
[SPEAKER_00]: By discerning these internally, it means by surmounting them internally, you abandon them internally.

126
00:12:02,053 --> 00:12:03,975
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind-stance still settles down.

127
00:12:04,135 --> 00:12:05,036
[SPEAKER_00]: It is unified.

128
00:12:05,156 --> 00:12:06,036
[SPEAKER_00]: It is composed.

129
00:12:06,497 --> 00:12:10,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Unification, composure, all result of proper mindfulness.

130
00:12:11,320 --> 00:12:12,361
[SPEAKER_00]: Peripheral anchoring.

131
00:12:13,482 --> 00:12:14,503
[SPEAKER_00]: Call it whichever way.

132
00:12:15,343 --> 00:12:20,527
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in this way, because a bhikkhu develops mindfulness of the body.

133
00:12:22,809 --> 00:12:24,050
[SPEAKER_00]: So, next page.

134
00:12:27,532 --> 00:12:28,113
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, it goes on.

135
00:12:28,833 --> 00:12:29,354
[SPEAKER_00]: Furthermore,

136
00:12:30,786 --> 00:12:33,387
[SPEAKER_00]: Bhikkhu walking understands I'm walking.

137
00:12:33,847 --> 00:12:45,071
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, while Bhikkhu is walking, he's aware of the significance, peripheral significance of his actual act of walking.

138
00:12:45,392 --> 00:12:47,192
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, he understands he's walking.

139
00:12:47,672 --> 00:12:52,314
[SPEAKER_00]: He's not telling to himself, walking, walking, touching, touching, moving, moving, feeling, feeling.

140
00:12:52,374 --> 00:12:59,177
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't need to, because all of those actions are possible to do on the peripheral level in the first place, because

141
00:12:59,797 --> 00:13:16,087
[SPEAKER_00]: The significance... sorry, all those actions are possible to do on the actual level, as in, you can attend to them, you can do it like this or like that, because on the peripheral level, a context, the significance of them is already present and remains present for for as long as you're doing it.

142
00:13:16,227 --> 00:13:16,908
[SPEAKER_00]: It endures.

143
00:13:18,689 --> 00:13:23,852
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he understands he's walking or if he's standing, he understands that he's standing.

144
00:13:24,614 --> 00:13:27,076
[SPEAKER_00]: If he's sitting down, he understands that he's sitting down.

145
00:13:27,136 --> 00:13:31,220
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, understanding sitting down means knowing to sit down.

146
00:13:31,240 --> 00:13:35,464
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean attending the act of sitting down.

147
00:13:35,545 --> 00:13:41,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Only person who is not free from doubt would be needing to attend something to keep knowing that he's doing it.

148
00:13:41,170 --> 00:13:44,233
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not knowledge, and knowledge won't arise from it.

149
00:13:44,253 --> 00:13:49,438
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a... that's what I... what I would be describing as a mechanical technique or a method.

150
00:13:50,574 --> 00:13:59,958
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's mechanical, because there's this assumed, almost like a blind faith, that as long as I keep looking, keep catching these moments, it will reveal the truth.

151
00:14:00,058 --> 00:14:01,378
[SPEAKER_00]: But the truth is in the knowledge.

152
00:14:01,578 --> 00:14:03,799
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha talks about it all the time.

153
00:14:06,620 --> 00:14:09,101
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, if he's lying down, he understands he's lying down.

154
00:14:09,901 --> 00:14:13,683
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, in whatever way his body is disposed, he understands this.

155
00:14:15,526 --> 00:14:21,528
[SPEAKER_00]: For one dwelling in this way, vigilant, ardent, resolute, memories and thoughts connected with home are abandoned.

156
00:14:23,049 --> 00:14:23,589
[SPEAKER_00]: And so on.

157
00:14:24,829 --> 00:14:25,450
[SPEAKER_00]: Then it goes on.

158
00:14:25,470 --> 00:14:29,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Bhikkhu going forward, going backward, he acts with awareness.

159
00:14:29,431 --> 00:14:35,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, awareness results in the understanding of the peripheral, because awareness in itself is properly anchored.

160
00:14:35,573 --> 00:14:40,855
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in relation... well, it's anchored in the peripheral, in relation to the actual.

161
00:14:41,475 --> 00:14:43,856
[SPEAKER_00]: Awareness is being basically discerning the relationship.

162
00:14:50,981 --> 00:14:54,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Looking forward, looking back, he's one who acts with awareness.

163
00:14:54,423 --> 00:14:56,964
[SPEAKER_00]: Bending, stretching, he's the one who acts with awareness.

164
00:14:58,881 --> 00:15:02,622
[SPEAKER_00]: Taking his saṅghati, ball and robe, he's the one who acts with awareness.

165
00:15:02,842 --> 00:15:06,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Eating, drinking, consuming, tasting, he's the one who acts with awareness.

166
00:15:06,863 --> 00:15:12,165
[SPEAKER_00]: Walking, standing, sitting down, sleeping, walking, speaking, being silent, he's the one who acts with awareness.

167
00:15:12,285 --> 00:15:17,666
[SPEAKER_00]: For one dwelling in this way, vigilant, ardent, resolute, any memories and thoughts connected with home are abandoned.

168
00:15:18,967 --> 00:15:24,448
[SPEAKER_00]: With abandoning of these internally, the mind stands still, settles down, it's unified, it's composed.

169
00:15:24,488 --> 00:15:27,109
[SPEAKER_00]: In this way, too, bhikkhu, the bhikkhu develops mindfulness of the body.

170
00:15:28,564 --> 00:15:31,485
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, goes on the same description, same passages, just changes.

171
00:15:31,605 --> 00:15:32,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Attending to the repulsive.

172
00:15:33,185 --> 00:15:34,426
[SPEAKER_00]: He attends to it with awareness.

173
00:15:34,486 --> 00:15:35,386
[SPEAKER_00]: Attending to the elements.

174
00:15:35,806 --> 00:15:37,646
[SPEAKER_00]: Attending to non-cemetery contemplation.

175
00:15:37,686 --> 00:15:40,247
[SPEAKER_00]: And you could see what's in common to these things.

176
00:15:40,287 --> 00:15:41,968
