355
00:32:46,461 --> 00:32:54,968
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people, when they would... back in the day, when the Buddha would describe what nibbāna is, they would freak out, fall on the ground, start ripping their hair out.

356
00:32:56,992 --> 00:33:01,336
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a genuine description of a terror, because for them, nibbāna is death.

357
00:33:02,497 --> 00:33:05,239
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for Putujana, nibbāna is death, basically.

358
00:33:05,359 --> 00:33:06,800
[SPEAKER_00]: Or what he thinks death is, basically.

359
00:33:07,801 --> 00:33:15,507
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh... Well, to add here, in order to be able to deal with existential anxiety, you have to be established in virtue.

360
00:33:19,210 --> 00:33:22,053
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the reason why people don't have enough mental strength,

361
00:33:22,978 --> 00:33:30,241
[SPEAKER_00]: To cope with the arisen existential dread and existential anxiety is because their behavior hasn't been purified beforehand, as the Buddha would say.

362
00:33:31,221 --> 00:33:47,848
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for somebody who's starting taking their virtue seriously, their precepts seriously, their restraint seriously, watching the speech, practicing non-harm, non-ill-will, and definitely non-sensuality, yeah, that is how you develop the mind so it can take the pain.

363
00:33:48,869 --> 00:33:51,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Any, any principle of strength.

364
00:33:52,155 --> 00:34:00,937
[SPEAKER_00]: You develop strength by exposing your weakness, by challenging it bit by bit by bit, and the mind adapts, the body adapts.

365
00:34:01,097 --> 00:34:02,297
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you become stronger.

366
00:34:05,158 --> 00:34:13,100
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the virtue has been developed beforehand, mindfulness of the body will reveal the great existential anxiety.

367
00:34:15,080 --> 00:34:18,141
[SPEAKER_00]: Mahato saṃvegāya saṃvatāti.

368
00:34:22,965 --> 00:34:26,606
[SPEAKER_00]: It will reveal the great existential anxiety that you will be able to hold.

369
00:34:27,966 --> 00:34:35,487
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you are able to hold it, then that mindfulness of the body leads to the next thing, which is the great benefit, great safety from bondage.

370
00:34:36,648 --> 00:34:43,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Leads to mindfulness and awareness, leads to attainment of knowledge, to pleasant dwelling in this very life, to freedom, to arahantship.

371
00:34:44,389 --> 00:34:46,189
[SPEAKER_00]: But not if you ignore it.

372
00:34:47,310 --> 00:34:49,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Not if you ignore the existential anxiety.

373
00:34:49,894 --> 00:34:52,096
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, that is indeed an interesting sutta.

374
00:34:52,256 --> 00:34:55,618
[SPEAKER_00]: Although, I'm sure if you find other English translations, they're going to be very different.

375
00:34:56,819 --> 00:34:58,340
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's just the nature of the suttas.

376
00:34:58,801 --> 00:35:08,888
[SPEAKER_00]: They're so general, so condensed in their meaning, that even if you read pure Pali, you're going to be putting your own views.

377
00:35:08,908 --> 00:35:16,874
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to be honest and you want to look for a context of your understanding that can then remain unchanged.

378
00:35:17,670 --> 00:35:33,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Regardless of the sutta you're reading, you want to basically start discerning the nature, the principles, that then no amount of sutta, or rather, if it's challenged by different suttas, means it's not adequate principle.

379
00:35:33,522 --> 00:35:38,085
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't just having a patchwork and just patching it as it arises, modifying it.

380
00:35:39,086 --> 00:35:41,888
[SPEAKER_00]: You want one sutta to represent the all suttas.

381
00:35:42,938 --> 00:35:52,000
[SPEAKER_00]: Insight into one... the nature of one sutta means you are able to understand all the other suttas if you pay attention, if you read them, if you sort of discern them.

382
00:35:52,581 --> 00:36:04,504
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... from that point of view, it will also become clear how it is possible to... how it was possible to become an arahant simply by one discourse by the Buddha, or even part of it.

383
00:36:05,144 --> 00:36:05,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

384
00:36:05,364 --> 00:36:09,845
[SPEAKER_00]: Because one contains the whole nature, the whole universal principle of the dhamma.

385
00:36:10,585 --> 00:36:12,366
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, that's what the Buddha said himself.

386
00:36:16,503 --> 00:36:19,686
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I think that should probably be enough for now.

387
00:36:19,966 --> 00:36:27,733
[SPEAKER_00]: There are quite a few more passages, but I could perhaps do a part two or three, because I think even this is more than enough for people to follow.

